<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marko's Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1345">1345</a>–<a href="/years/1377">1377</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Markov Manastir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marko's Monastery is a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery of Saint Demetrius in the village of Markova Sušica near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Its church was begun in 1345 by Vukašin and completed around 1376–77 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a>, whose portraits appear among its frescoes. It is one of the best-preserved monuments of 14th-century Byzantine-style painting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/markos-monastery.jpg" alt="Marko's Monastery" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Georgievski from Skopje, Macedonia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marko-Monastery-church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The monastery stands in the village of Markova Sušica, 18 kilometres from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, and has been in use since it was founded. It takes its name from the Serbian Prince Marko, who reigned when the building was completed in the 14th century. Its single church, dedicated to Saint Demetrius, is built on a cross plan of brick and stone, with a narthex, a central dome and a smaller dome to the west; the iconostasis is made of stone pillars. Around it stand lodgings, a belfry, a well, warehouses, a bakery and a mill, and the monks still keep a special oven for making rakija.</p><p>The frescoes were the work of several painters from the region. Among their subjects are the Holy Mother of God, the twelve great feasts, Jesus Christ and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, and the founders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">King Vukašin</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a> appear as ktetors, the donors of the church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before Ottoman rule the monastery kept a school, and its monks and priests copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> came under Ottoman rule in 1392, and many churches and monasteries of the area were destroyed, but Marko's Monastery suffered almost no damage. An Ottoman record of 1467/8 counts 20 monks there. Kiril Peychinovich was its hegumen, or abbot, from 1801 to 1818, and in 1830 the Ottoman aristocrat Hamzi Paşa added an exonarthex, an outer porch, to the church.</p><p>In 1894 the Bulgarian metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> had frescoes overpainted with a fat-based paint, an act of national fanaticism that most damaged the portraits of Vukašin and Marko. Attempts to restore the damaged paintings were made in the 1920s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marko's Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marko became an Ottoman vassal, minted coins and completed <b>Marko's Monastery</b> near Skopje, begun by his father.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li></ul></section>
